<h2>Running the recall application well</h2>
<p>The proviso application is a mini-trial, and it is won on documents and immediacy. The challenger should move at the first discovery of the vitiating fact, explain any delay by affidavit, and place primary materials: the vakalatnama&rsquo;s scope where counsel&rsquo;s authority is disputed, handwriting comparison requests where signatures are denied, the correspondence trail where coercion is alleged. Courts test these claims hard — a consent decree represents reliance by the other side, often acted upon through payments, possession changes or withdrawn proceedings, and restitution complexity grows by the month. The respondent defending the decree should prove the settlement&rsquo;s negotiation history: drafts exchanged, presence in court, post-decree conduct consistent with acceptance — the most eloquent answer to a belated repudiation.</p>

<h2>Consent decrees in matrimonial and commercial practice</h2>
<p>Two contexts generate most of the litigation. In matrimonial settlements, decrees framed on consent terms — alimony, custody arrangements, withdrawal of prosecutions — are occasionally attacked when one spouse&rsquo;s circumstances change; the discipline above applies, and changed circumstances justify variation only where the decree or statute (as with maintenance and custody, which remain inherently variable) so provides, never a free appeal. In commercial matters, consent decrees and decrees on compromise recorded in mediation settlements enjoy the same protection; a party alleging that the mediated agreement was mis-recorded must return to the referring court promptly, with the signed settlement as the touchstone.</p>
<div class="check">
  <p>Before consenting: read the terms as a contract, because that is what survives — and insist the decree annex the signed terms verbatim.</p>
  <p>After discovering a vice: move the decreeing court immediately; parallel suits are barred and delay is read as acceptance.</p>
  <p>Defending a decree: compile the reliance record early — payments made, possession delivered, cases withdrawn.</p>
  <p>Advising strangers: a consent decree binds its parties; independent rights are litigated independently.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer states general principles for information only and is not advice in any particular matter.</div>
